<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <!--
            allowBackup is false, and deliberately.

            Android's automatic backup copies an application's private storage -
            here, the OPFS directory holding the entire SQLite database - to the
            user's Google Drive. For an application whose stated promise is that
            data never leaves the device, leaving that on would make the promise
            false, quietly, by default, on the first idle overnight charge.

            The cost is that a new phone does not inherit the data. That cost is
            already covered: Settings has an explicit backup export that writes
            a file the user chooses to keep, and an import that reads it back.
            A backup you performed is worth more than one you assumed.

            The AI assistant does not soften this. A question may travel; the
            database still has no route to Google Drive or anywhere else.
        -->

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
        Permissions requested from the system: three, and this comment has been
        wrong twice already.

        It once said none. That ended with the AI assistant: a person pastes
        their own Anthropic key into Settings and asks a question, and answering
        it means reaching https://api.anthropic.com. On Android nothing reaches
        anything without INTERNET. There is no per-host form of the permission,
        no way to hold it only while a feature is switched on, and no way to
        hand a release build a key it can use without one.

        It then said "RECORD_AUDIO IS NOT HERE, AND THE MICROPHONE WORKS. Do not
        add RECORD_AUDIO." That was true of the design and false of the phone.
        The paragraph below replaces it.

        RECORD_AUDIO, AND WHY THE PERMISSION-FREE DESIGN WAS ABANDONED

        Speech used to arrive through ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH: the system's own
        screen opened, the system held the microphone, and this application was
        handed a sentence. It opened no audio stream, so it needed no
        permission. That was the better design and it is gone.

        It is gone because on a moto g35 5G - the phone this application is
        built for - that Intent answers "Voice search isn't available". The
        component that handles it, Google Voice Search, is not available on that
        device. Four releases were spent on the far side of that door, including
        an online retry that knocked on the same one.

        The keyboard's voice typing works perfectly on the same phone. That is
        the fact that settled it: the device can transcribe, it just will not
        serve the request in that shape. Gboard does not fire the Intent - it
        binds the recognition service directly through SpeechRecognizer, and
        that API records in this process, so it needs RECORD_AUDIO.

        The user was asked and accepted the permission in order to have a
        microphone that works. It is requested at runtime on the first press of
        the microphone and never at startup, and a refusal is an outcome the
        interface handles rather than an error - see SpeechPlugin.

        POST_NOTIFICATIONS, AND THE THREE THAT CAME WITH IT AND WERE SENT BACK

        This application now schedules expiry reminders, and from Android 13
        nothing may post a notification without POST_NOTIFICATIONS. It is asked
        for at runtime the first time somebody switches the reminders ON, in
        Settings, and never at startup - so a person who does not want to be
        interrupted is never shown the dialog. The switch ships off. See
        src/services/notifications/notifier.ts.

        @capacitor/local-notifications merges FOUR permissions into this
        manifest, not one. Three of them are removed above rather than
        accepted, and each removal is a decision with a cost:

          SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM. The plugin defaults every notification to an
          exact alarm, and on Android 12 and later that opens the system's
          "Alarms & reminders" screen the first time it schedules - a second
          permission screen, for a daily digest about tinned food. Every notice
          this application schedules sets isExactNotification false, so the
          alarm is inexact and the permission is not needed. What it costs is
          that a reminder set for 09:00 may arrive at 09:04. Nothing here needs
          a stopwatch.

          RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED. The plugin registers a receiver that
          re-registers pending alarms after a restart, because Android drops
          every alarm on reboot. Without the permission that receiver never
          runs, so a phone that restarts loses the reminders it was holding
          until this application is next opened - which recomputes and
          reschedules the lot. THIS IS THE ONE REAL COST OF KEEPING THE
          PERMISSION LIST SHORT, and it is written down here and in
          docs/ANDROID.md rather than discovered. The receiver stays in the
          merged manifest and is simply never delivered to.

          WAKE_LOCK. Nothing in the plugin's code takes a wake lock; the
          declaration is inherited. RTC_WAKEUP alarms wake the device through
          the alarm manager's own lock, not the application's, so removing this
          changes nothing that can be observed.

        The reminders still arrive on a sleeping phone: the schedule sets
        allowWhileIdle, which fires during Doze and needs no permission of any
        kind. The channel is created without vibration, which is why
        android.permission.VIBRATE is not here either.

        WHAT IS STILL TRUE, AND STILL CHECKED

        The check in .github/workflows/android.yml was narrowed, not deleted. It
        allows exactly android.permission.INTERNET,
        android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO and
        android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS, and still fails the build on
        every other permission outside this application's own namespace: CAMERA,
        location, contacts, storage, the three removed above, and anything a
        later dependency drags in. It used to prove "this application asks the
        system for nothing". It now proves "this application asks for the
        network, the microphone and permission to notify you, and nothing
        else", which is a weaker sentence and still worth checking on every
        build, because the way this promise would actually be lost is one
        library quietly merging one more permission.

        RECORD_AUDIO is a microphone, not a network. What is recorded on the
        offline attempt is transcribed by an on-device service and never leaves
        the phone; the second attempt, which the user can switch off entirely,
        is made by the system's own recognizer over its own connection. Neither
        travels through this application, and the audio is never stored - see
        docs/VOICE.md and docs/OFFLINE.md.

        INTERNET remains what the assistant needs, and what used to be proved by
        its absence is still proved in three narrower places, each of which
        fails closed:

          - index.html allows exactly one host, and connect-src refuses the
            rest at the browser.
          - scripts/audit-offline.mjs allows that host in exactly one module,
            src/services/ai/client.ts, and fails the build on any other
            external URL anywhere.
          - With no key stored, and with the assistant switched off, nothing
            runs and nothing is sent. That is the shipping default.

        The merged manifest also carries one entry added by androidx:
        DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION, namespaced under this
        application's own id. It is a permission this application defines to
        keep other applications away from its broadcast receivers, not one it
        asks anybody for. The workflow allows that name and fails on any other.

        None of this changes how the application is loaded. Capacitor serves it
        from https://localhost through WebViewAssetLoader, which reads the
        APK's own assets and never opens a socket. Every asset is still inside
        the package, and the first launch still works with the radio off.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        Merged in by @capacitor/local-notifications and taken back out. The
        argument for each is in the block above; the short version is that an
        inexact alarm needs no exact-alarm permission, nothing here takes a wake
        lock, and losing the schedule across a reboot costs one recompute the
        next time the application is opened.

        tools:node="remove" acts at merge time, so these three never reach the
        APK and the check in the workflow never sees them. Deleting these lines
        would let all three back in silently, and the build would then fail -
        which is the intended way to find out.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
        Not a permission, and the only reason it is here.

        From Android 11 an application sees no other application it has not
        named. SpeechPlugin asks SpeechRecognizer.isRecognitionAvailable whether
        anything on this phone transcribes speech, and then binds the service it
        finds; both the question and the bind go through package visibility, so
        without this the microphone would report itself unavailable on every
        modern phone.

        It names the service rather than the Intent now. The activity action
        android.speech.action.RECOGNIZE_SPEECH used to be here too, because the
        plugin fired that Intent. It no longer does - see the RECORD_AUDIO note
        above - and a query for something nothing asks about is a line that
        cannot be checked.

        This grants nothing and asks the system for nothing. It is not
        QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES, which is a permission and is not here; it makes
        visible only the applications that themselves declare they can do speech
        recognition. The check in the workflow still finds nothing but INTERNET
        and RECORD_AUDIO.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.RecognitionService" />
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
